{"id":"q16","run":1,"role":"calibration","category":"probability","question":"A family has two children, and you learn that at least one of them is a boy. Assuming each child is independently equally likely to be a boy or a girl, is the probability that both are boys 1/3?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":null,"claims":[{"text":"The four outcomes BB, BG, GB, GG are equally likely, each with probability 1/4.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Learning at least one child is a boy eliminates only the GG outcome.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The remaining outcomes BB, BG, GB are equally likely, and only one of the three is BB, so the probability is 1/3.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The other child is a boy with probability 1/2 because it is independent of the first child.","confidence":0.1,"lean":-1,"theta":0.1571,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The independence argument only applies if a specific child's identity is known, such as the older child being a boy.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Knowing 'at least one is a boy' is a statement about the pair, not about a particular child.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The question gives no detail on how the information was learned, so the four-outcome enumeration is the correct model.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"If the information were 'the older child is a boy', the probability would be 1/2.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The question does not describe a different information procedure that would change the answer.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relat